EXT. OAK CREEK ELEMENTARY - PLAYGROUND - DAY

The afternoon sun shines bright. The school yard is wide and green.

Zayan, a nine-year-old South Asian boy, sprints across the grass. He wears a red hoodie with a frayed left cuff and scuffed white sneakers. His tight dark curls bounce.

He runs in a wild, zigzag pattern. He leaps over a low wooden bench. He does a quick cartwheel on the grass.

He stops, breathing hard. He grins. The hyperactive energy in his legs feels a little lighter now.

Zayan looks toward the heavy double doors of the school. He reaches into his pocket. It is empty.

ZAYAN
My bounce-ball. Still in the science closet.

He walks quickly toward the school entrance.

INT. OAK CREEK ELEMENTARY - MAIN CORRIDOR - DAY

The hallway is quiet. The yellow lockers stand in neat rows.

Zayan stops in front of Locker 112. He stares at the combination lock. He blinks.

ZAYAN
Wait. Was it twenty-four? Or forty-two?

He rubs his forehead. His short-term memory feels like a blank chalkboard.

He wears a thick red rubber band around his left wrist. He pulls it back and snaps it.

Snap!

The sharp pinch brings him back to the moment.

ZAYAN
Right. Forty-two.

He spins the dial. The locker clicks open. He grabs his backpack.

Mr. Henderson, a tall teacher with gray hair and a blue sweater, walks down the hall. Beside him is Leo, a classmate wearing a striped shirt.

MR. HENDERSON
Zayan? The school day is over. Why are you still here?

ZAYAN
I left my special glowing green bounce-ball in the science closet, Mr. Henderson. I need it for the weekend.

LEO
It is just a ball, Zayan.

Zayan shakes his head.

ZAYAN
It is not just a ball. It bounces six feet high.

Suddenly, the floor rumbles. A deep grind echoes through the walls.

The yellow lockers slide to the left. The tiled floor shifts like a conveyor belt.

Mr. Henderson grabs the wall.

MR. HENDERSON
What is happening?

The ceiling panels rotate. A thick gray wall drops from above, blocking the front exit.

The corridor is no longer straight. It is a branching path.

LEO
The school! It is turning into a giant puzzle!

Zayan looks down the newly formed hallway. Three different paths open up.

INT. OAK CREEK ELEMENTARY - CORRIDOR MAZE - DAY

The walls hum. Blue and orange light strips pulse along the floor.

Mr. Henderson runs to the gray wall and pushes it. It does not budge.

MR. HENDERSON
We are locked in. The security system must have glitched.

Leo looks at his watch.

LEO
The walls just moved again! Look!

The left corridor slides shut. A new one opens on the right.

Zayan watches the movement. His eyes d_art from the ceiling to the floor. His brain takes in the colors, the speed, the sound of the gears.

ZAYAN
They shift every thirty seconds. Exactly thirty.

MR. HENDERSON
How do we get out? The main office is on the other side of the building.

LEO
We need to map it.

Leo pulls out a notepad. He draws a line.

LEO
We go straight, then left, then--

The wall beside Leo slides forward. His map is instantly useless.

LEO
Never mind. We are doomed.

Zayan steps forward. His eyes track the ceiling tiles. He sees three steps ahead.

ZAYAN
We are not doomed. We just need to move fast. Follow me.

Zayan runs down the middle corridor. Mr. Henderson and Leo exchange a look, then run after him.

INT. OAK CREEK ELEMENTARY - SHIFTING HALLWAY - DAY

The group runs. The air is cool.

Zayan stops at a crossroads. He looks left. He looks right.

In his mind, three different paths map themselves out like glowing lines.

Path A leads to the cafeteria, but the gears are moving too fast. Path B leads to a dead end. Path C will open in exactly five seconds.

ZAYAN
Stop here!

MR. HENDERSON
Zayan, we should keep moving!

ZAYAN
Trust me. Five, four, three, two, one...

The wall in front of them slides upward. A bright hallway appears.

LEO
Whoa! How did you know that?

Zayan shrugs. He keeps his pace fast.

ZAYAN
The blue lights pulse slower before the wall lifts. It is a pattern.

LEO
Your brain works so fast. It is like a superpower!

Zayan stops. He looks at Leo. His face is serious.

ZAYAN
It is not a superpower, Leo. Having ADHD means my brain sees everything all at once. It is super hard. Sometimes I cannot focus on what the teacher is saying. I forget my lunch. I lose my shoes. My head feels like a balloon ready to pop.

Leo blinks. He looks down at his sneakers.

LEO
Oh. I did not realize it was that tough.

ZAYAN
It is. But right now, my fast brain is exactly what we need.

Mr. Henderson looks at his watch.

MR. HENDERSON
Ten seconds before the next shift! Which way, Zayan?

Zayan looks at the three doors ahead.

ZAYAN
The green door!

They sprint through the green door just as a heavy metal barrier slides down behind them.

INT. OAK CREEK ELEMENTARY - CENTRAL VAULT - DAY

They stand in a large, circular room. This is the center of the school.

In the middle of the room is the high-shelf storage vault. High up on a metal shelf sits Zayan's glowing green bounce-ball.

The exit door is across the room. It is blocked by three concentric metal rings. The rings spin in opposite directions.

MR. HENDERSON
The control panel is broken. We cannot stop the rings.

Leo looks at the spinning metal.

LEO
They are moving too fast. If we try to squeeze through, we will get stuck.

Zayan walks closer. He snaps his rubber band.

Snap!

He stares at the rings. They spin.

Ring one rotates clockwise every four seconds. Ring two rotates counter-clockwise every six seconds. Ring three rotates clockwise every eight seconds.

Zayan sees the intersection. There is a tiny window where all three openings align.

ZAYAN
They align. Every twenty-four seconds. But only for two seconds.

MR. HENDERSON
Two seconds? That is not enough time to get all of us through!

ZAYAN
It is if we run together.

Zayan looks at the high shelf. He sees his glowing green bounce-ball.

ZAYAN
I need to get my ball first.

MR. HENDERSON
Zayan, there is no time!

Zayan climbs a nearby ladder. He is quick. He reaches out and grabs the glowing green ball. He slips it into his hoodie pocket.

The gears in the walls begin to grind louder. The walls of the vault are starting to close in.

LEO
The whole room is shrinking! Zayan, get down!

Zayan slides down the ladder. He lands on his feet.

He stands before the spinning rings.

ZAYAN
We have one shot. When I say go, we run. No stopping.

Mr. Henderson looks nervous. Leo holds his breath.

Zayan tracks the rings. He counts the pulses of light on the floor.

ZAYAN
Three... two... one... GO!

Zayan grabs Leo's hand. Mr. Henderson follows close behind.

They dive through the rings.

The metal circles spin past their shoulders.

They slide across the floor on the other side.

CLANG!

The rings lock shut behind them.

EXT. OAK CREEK ELEMENTARY - PLAYGROUND - LATER

The heavy double doors of the school burst open.

Zayan, Leo, and Mr. Henderson stumble out onto the sunny playground.

Behind them, the school doors lock with a quiet beep. The security system resets.

Mr. Henderson sits on the grass, catching his breath.

MR. HENDERSON
We made it. We actually made it.

Leo looks at Zayan.

LEO
You were amazing, Zayan. You saw things none of us could see.

Zayan pulls the glowing green bounce-ball from his pocket. He tosses it into the air and catches it.

ZAYAN
I just wanted my ball.

Leo smiles.

LEO
Well, you got it.

Zayan bounces the ball on the sidewalk. It flies high into the air, over the bench, and back into his hand.

His legs are still full of energy. He starts to jog in place. He snaps his rubber band one more time.

Snap!

He looks at the wide open soccer field.

ZAYAN
I am going to run three laps before my mom gets here.

Zayan sprints across the grass, fast and free, his mind already racing onto the next big thing.